AB  - Remaining Useful Life prediction is vital for equipment health management. Although Transformer models perform well in this area, their quadratic complexity and weak local sensitivity limit their effectiveness on long sequences. To overcome this, we construct a Temporal Adjacent Attention mechanism by introducing a local bias into the linear attention framework. Specifically, a Manhattan distance modulated by a cosine function is embedded into the query-key similarity computation, enabling the model to capture local temporal dependencies more effectively. Furthermore, incorporate Feature Squeeze and Feature Retention Connections to mitigate the impact of feature dimensions while preserving important features, leading to the design of the Temporal Adjacent Attention block. Finally, propose the Temporal Adjacent Transformer model. To evaluate its effectiveness, conducted two comparative evaluations on the commercial modular aero-propulsion system simulation (C-MAPSS) dataset. First, compared Temporal Adjacent Attention with several existing attention mechanisms, demonstrating that it achieves lower computational complexity and superior performance. Second, comparative evaluation with state-of-the-art models and the standard Transformer demonstrates that the Temporal Adjacent Transformer achieves 39.44 % reduction in parameter count and 12.41 % decrease in Root-Mean-Square Error (RMSE), thereby striking an effective balance between predictive accuracy and computational complexity. The generalization performance of the model is verified on the other two datasets.
