KW  - Weather forecasting
KW  - Spatio-temporal Mamba
KW  - Wavelet transform
AB  - The spatio-temporal evolution process of weather conditions is driven by stable and fluctuating patterns. However, existing weather forecasting methods usually depend on a single pattern to learn spatio-temporal features, which may result in the introduction of spurious spatio-temporal dependencies and hinder the forecasting performance. To address the issue, based on the insight that stable and fluctuating patterns lie in low- and high-frequency components in the wavelet domain, we propose a joint framework of wavelet transform with Mamba for stable and fluctuating patterns in spatio-temporal weather forecasting. Specifically, this method first performs the discrete wavelet transform to obtain the low- and high-frequency components along the temporal and spatial dimensions, respectively. Then, in the temporal dimension, a temporal learner is proposed to capture long-term stable trends and short-term fluctuation dynamics from temporal low- and high-frequency components through tailored sliding enhanced long-term Mamba (SETM) and energy guided short-term Mamba (EGSM). On this basis, a temporal frequency mixture gate (FMG) is designed to adaptively incorporate these two characteristics, yielding temporal features aligned with genuine temporal characteristics. Concurrently, in the spatial dimension, a spatial learner is constructed to leverage customized sliding enhanced global Mamba (SEGM) and region perceiving Mamba (RPM) for respectively modeling global and local spatial correlations hidden in spatial low- and high-frequency components. Besides, a spatial frequency mixture gate (FMG) dynamically aggregates them to obtain spatial features with comprehensive spatial perception capabilities. Extensive experiments on two global high-resolution weather datasets achieve state-of-the-art performance against mainstream approaches.
