AB  - Resistance spot welding is a key process in body-in-white manufacturing, where weld quality directly impacts the safety of the vehicle. Compared to traditional single-pulse welding, multi-pulse spot welding enables more refined control over nugget growth through staged heating and cooling, thereby enhancing weld quality. However, multi-pulse welding signals exhibit significantly more complex structures, characterized by multi-peak fluctuations and abrupt transitions, and are often contaminated with non-stationary noise induced by varying welding conditions. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a novel anomaly detection model for multi-pulse welding signals—Conditional Denoising Differential Variational Autoencoder (C-DDVAE). Built upon the variational autoencoder architecture, the model integrates the Efficient–Frequency Interleaved Attention module (EFIAM) to suppress non-stationary noise in shallow encoded features caused by process disturbances, while enhancing the expression of welding trends in deeper features to extract key representations of nugget growth. Furthermore, the model incorporates the Conditional Multi-Head Differential Attention module (C-MHDA), which explicitly models the rate of change across time steps using a multi-head differential attention mechanism, thereby improving its capability to capture multi-peak and abrupt characteristics. In addition, considering the staged nature of the multi-pulse welding process, a process-prior-informed masking mechanism and a physics-based energy-weighted loss function are designed to guide the model's focus toward critical physical information and effective feature regions. Experimental results show that C-DDVAE achieves an anomaly detection accuracy of 95.82 %, surpassing existing models. These results validate the effectiveness of the proposed method, making it well-suited for online quality inspection in automated automotive production lines.
