AB  - This study investigates the three-dimensional flow of a Williamson–micropolar nanofluid over a bi-directionally stretched surface under the influence of a magnetic field. The heat and mass transfer behavior is analyzed by incorporating the effects of Brownian motion and thermophoresis. The governing partial differential equations are converted into nonlinear ordinary differential equations using similarity transformations and solved through a hybrid artificial intelligence framework that combines a global genetic algorithm with a local active set method. The results are verified using the three-stage Lobatto IIIa implicit Runge–Kutta method. Regularization terms (L1 and L2) are included in the cost function to improve stability, and Monte Carlo simulations are performed under 1%–5% Gaussian noise to test robustness. The results show that the magnetic field reduces both axial and transverse velocities due to the Lorentz force, while larger Williamson parameters suppress velocity because of fluid elasticity. The Brownian motion parameter increases temperature but decreases concentration, whereas the thermophoresis factor thickens the thermal boundary layer by promoting nanoparticle migration. A higher Lewis number leads to lower concentration because of reduced mass diffusivity. Regression analysis of engineering quantities such as the Nusselt number, Sherwood number, and skin friction coefficients reveals that Gaussian process regression and support vector regression provide the highest predictive accuracy. The proposed hybrid solver demonstrates excellent agreement with numerical solutions, confirming its accuracy, efficiency, and robustness for simulating complex Williamson–micropolar nanofluid flow systems. Overall, the study identifies magnetic intensity, Brownian motion, thermophoresis, and Lewis number as key parameters influencing thermal and concentration gradients. The hybrid solver ensures consistent and stable predictions, highlighting its effectiveness for modeling coupled heat and mass transfer in micropolar nanofluids.
