AB  - Medical image processing is essential for accurate diagnosis and optimal treatment planning. However, noisy, intricate anatomical structures and blurry tumor borders frequently pose serious challenges for traditional segmentation techniques. This work presents a segmentation method called the k-way Z-Intuitionistic Normalized Hypergraph Cut algorithm (k-Zinhg), a machine learning-based segmentation framework that integrates intuitionistic fuzzy sets with spectral hypergraph clustering for brain Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) tumor detection. The proposed technique ensures robust tumor region segmentation by integrating intuitionistic fuzzy logic’s hesitation degrees in a unique way with model uncertainty. Superpixels are used to reduce computational complexity without sacrificing essential structural information and a k-way normalized hypergraph cut is employed to efficiently partition the image into meaningful regions. In order to provide a strong framework for brain tumor segmentation, the suggested k-Zinhg algorithm is unique in that it can concurrently capture uncertainty through Z-intuitionistic fuzzy sets, preserve structural details via superpixels and achieve consistent accuracy with k-way hypergraph partitioning. Extensive tests on the publicly available Brain MRI 35 High-Resolution (Br35H) brain tumor dataset demonstrate outstanding performance with accuracy values ranging from 84.50% to 97.57%, significantly surpassing conventional methods such as Otsu’s, Kittler’s, k-Means, Fuzzy C-Means Clustering (FCM) and Hypergraph Normalized Cut (HNC) approaches. Notably the algorithm achieves high accuracy, demonstrating its reliability for medical image processing. Future work will focus on extending this approach to multimodal imaging such as Computed Tomography (CT) and Positron Emission Tomography (PET) as well as real-time medical deployment with reduced computational complexity for surgical applications.
