AB  - As science and industry develop and the knowledge level of users improves, the power usage scenarios of users have become increasingly complex, and the abnormal electricity usage behaviors of users have tended to be more intricate, concealed, and intelligent. Traditional artificial intelligence methods face challenges in accuracy and timeliness in detecting anomalous user power usage behavior. To solve the problem of quickly and accurately identifying the anomalous electricity utilization behavior of consumers, this research proposes a large model in the professional field, i.e., google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network model. The proposed google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network applies a parallel fusion approach with the lightweight google inception network, the lightweight residual network - 18, and the lightweight bidirectional encoder representations from transformers model. The google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network architecture proposed in this study first fuses the lightweight google inception network and lightweight residual network - 18 in parallel, then connects to the lightweight BERT. The proposed google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network adopts a parallel architecture, feeding the outputs of two pre-trained neural networks into a lightweight bidirectional encoder representations from transformers model instead of a simple concatenation, thereby enabling rapid and accurate identification of abnormal user power consumption behavior. The parallel fusion approach and lightweight structure enable the google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network to read complex feature information from a small amount of data with faster training speed and higher accuracy. In this study, 16 neural networks, including the google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network, are trained and tested employing column D and column S data from the Almanac of Minutely Power Dataset Version 2 along with the northern Italy dataset. Under the test on the Class D dataset, the accuracy of the google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network is 34 % higher than the second-place places365 - google inception network. Under the test on the Class S dataset, google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network ranks first in training speed (151 s faster than the second-ranked Residual Network-18) and achieves an accuracy that is 8.05 % higher than that of the second-ranked Residual Network-101. Under the test on the northern Italy dataset, google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network trained 27.07 % faster than the second-place google residual network 3 and achieved 7.67 % higher test accuracy than the second-place google residual network 3. Comparative experimental results of 16 neural networks show that the proposed google residual bidirectional encoder representations from transformers network is characterized by high accuracy and speed.
