AB  - As one of the world’s three major grains, wheat is widely cultivated across the globe. The airborne transmission of wheat stripe rust makes it widely distributed and highly contagious, posing a serious threat to global food security. Timely control can effectively curb the spread of stripe rust and prevent environmental pollution caused by the overuse of chemical agents. This paper proposes a deep learning framework based on spectral data that uses the Normalized Red-Edge1 Index (NREDI1) vegetation index, calculated from the rust-sensitive bands, to identify diseased wheat plots. Wheat planting areas are extracted from the computed NREDI1 imagery to prevent interference from non-wheat areas in rust detection. Then, the extracted imagery is processed through a predictive model using deep learning to uncover latent period information within the spectral data, facilitating the prediction of wheat stripe rust occurrence. In addition, Spatiotemporal Cell Memory In Memory (STC-MIM) was proposed, which modifies the underlying Spatiotemporal Long Short Term Memory (ST-LSTM) module in the existing MIM model by merging the cell state into the memory state at the network’s base layer. This allows information within neural network cells to propagate horizontally and vertically through the memory stream. This model outperformed all other predictive models, achieving a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 2.713 and a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 79.729. This framework demonstrates the use of neural networks and satellite data to predict wheat stripe rust spread trends, offering valuable insights for agricultural disease prevention.
