AB  - Seismic inversion is essential for subsurface characterization, including reservoir fluid discrimination, with applications in hydrocarbon exploration, geological carbon dioxide (CO2) sequestration, and environmental monitoring. Traditional methods, while physically grounded, are computationally demanding and sensitive to subsurface heterogeneity. Conversely, purely data-driven deep learning models often lack interpretability and perform poorly in data-limited settings. To address these limitations, this study presents a hybrid inversion framework that integrates deep learning with geostatistics and physics-based constraints. Terrain-following meshes, constructed from interpreted seismic horizons, guide three-dimensional (3D) synthetic porosity modeling via sequential Gaussian simulation, constrained by well-log data. Four fully connected neural networks are trained to learn nonlinear mappings between seismic attributes and elastic parameters—acoustic impedance, the ratio of compressional and shear wave velocities, and Poisson's ratio—as proxies for lithofluid content. The networks incorporate Gassmann's fluid substitution theory and Aki-Richards approximations. An optimized ensemble, developed via random weight search and enhanced through higher-order statistical uncertainty quantification, demonstrates high predictive performance on field seismic data (e.g., Coefficient of Determination “R2” = 0.947; Mean Squared Error “MSE” = 0.00112), with narrow uncertainty bounds. Evaluation metrics—including cross-plots, Taylor diagrams, and residual histograms—show strong agreement with well-log data (Pearson Correlation Coefficient “R” = 0.8907, probability value “p-value” ≈ 3.46 × 10−110), while minimal skewness (−0.136) and kurtosis (0.142) indicate unbiased and stable predictions. Despite limitations in highly heterogeneous formations due to simplified petroelastic assumptions, the proposed method offers a scalable, interpretable, and computationally efficient solution—achieving full inversion in 3 min on a Graphics Processing Unit (GPU). This contributes to enhanced reservoir hydrocarbon resource characterization and robust CO2 storage monitoring, which is paramount within the context of energy transition.
