AB  - This paper proposes a multimodal adaptive Bayesian quadrature active learning method (MABQ) that integrates Gaussian mixture model (GMM) with Bayesian failure probability inference (BFPI) to address reliability analysis under multimodal uncertainty conditions. The methodology first establishes a probabilistic characterization system for multimodal random variables based on GMM. Within the BFPI framework, it then derives analytical solutions for multimodal global posterior parameters to quantify multimodal uncertainty. Furthermore, the study develops an augmented multimodal global upper-bound posterior variance contribution (AMGUPVC) learning function, which incorporates modal weight adaptation mechanisms and a multimodal dual stopping criterion to achieve efficient detection of failure boundaries. Systematic validation through numerical examples and engineering case studies demonstrates that the proposed method exhibits superior accuracy and computational efficiency compared to conventional approaches in multimodal distribution scenarios, effectively identifying complex system failure characteristics while demonstrating significant potential for practical engineering reliability analysis applications.
