AB  - Multivariate time series forecasting is crucial across various critical domains, including weather prediction, finance, traffic management, and industrial manufacturing. While deep learning models based on the Transformer Neural Network (Transformer) and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) have recently shown remarkable performance, they often suffer from structural uniformity and fixed-scale designs. These limitations hinder their ability to fully capture the complex, dynamic, and multi-scale nature of real-world time series data. To overcome these limitations, this paper introduces a novel deep learning architecture: the Dual-Branch Multi-Scale Transformer-MLP (DBMS). The DBMS effectively integrates the Transformer’s capability of modeling long-range dependencies and global dynamics with the MLP’s advantage in capturing local patterns and short-term fluctuations. Specifically, the model incorporates a coarse-to-fine multi-scale design to perform hierarchical feature extraction across multiple temporal resolutions, facilitating more structured temporal modeling that captures both global trends and local variations. Furthermore, the proposed dynamic multi-scale fusion mechanism ensures collaborative perception and robust representation of temporal patterns across different scales. Extensive experiments on eight real-world datasets demonstrate that DBMS significantly outperforms existing models based solely on Transformer or MLP, achieving state-of-the-art performance with superior robustness and stability.
