AB  - This paper presents an explainable artificial intelligence framework that leverages Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) regression to model and optimize the precision micro-drilling of a novel carbon dot-reinforced epoxy nanocomposite. The XGBoost algorithm was specifically chosen for its proven efficacy in capturing complex, non-linear relationships in high-dimensional parameter spaces, achieving high predictive accuracy for thrust force, hole roundness, and damage area (R2test up to 0.98). A full-factorial Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) statistically validated the significance of machining parameters, identifying tool diameter as the most dominant factor (p < 0.001) and revealing critical two-factor interactions. To further transform this predictive model into a tool for physical insight, the Extended Fourier Amplitude Sensitivity Test (EFAST) was integrated, moving beyond a black-box to provide robust, model-agnostic sensitivity analysis. This hybrid methodology quantified tool diameter and nanofiller content as dominant factors, with model predictions and statistical post-hoc analysis confirming that 1 wt% carbon dots significantly enhance machinability by reducing thrust force and damage area. These AI-driven findings were physically validated via physicochemical characterization, linking performance gains to improved matrix–filler interactions. Finally, multi-objective optimization performed on the XGBoost model identified optimal parameters (2500 rpm, 10 mm/min, 0.30 mm, 1 wt%), achieving a composite desirability of 0.971. The primary contribution is this validated AI framework, which demonstrates how XGBoost, complemented by rigorous statistical validation, serves as a powerful and interpretable core for optimizing complex manufacturing processes.
