AB  - Current adversarial sample generation methods based on generative adversarial networks (GANs) (e.g., AdvGAN and AdvGAN++) face challenges in generator representation, parameter efficiency, and feature utilization, which result in adversarial samples with weak attack strength and poor stealthiness. To address these limitations, this paper proposes the Adversarial Ghost U-shaped Generative Adversarial Network (AdvGUG), which advances artificial intelligence (AI) through a dual-channel feature fusion design and a hybrid dilated convolution attention mechanism (HDC). Specifically, the generator has two streams: a compact U-shaped encoder that captures features at multiple scales, and a companion stream that mines perturbation cues from AdvGAN samples; they are fused through skip connections to construct targeted, high-strength, and imperceptible perturbations, while an HDC attention module enhances multi-scale feature fusion and robustness. Benefiting from this design, AdvGUG achieves optimized parameter efficiency, reduces computational redundancy, and in engineering applications, it is suitable for deployment in resource-constrained environments. Experimental evaluations on Tiny-ImageNet and the Canadian Institute For Advanced Research dataset with 10 classes (CIFAR-10) show that AdvGUG attains targeted attack success rates of 98.42 % against Residual Network 34 (ResNet-34), representing a 4.05 % improvement over AdvGAN, and 99.03 % against Residual Network 18 (ResNet-18). In addition, in black-box transfer attacks, AdvGUG reaches a 95.89 % success rate against EfficientNet-B0, outperforming both AdvGAN and AdvGAN++, thereby demonstrating enhanced attack efficiency and stealthiness for real-world AI system testing.
