AB  - Financial time series forecasting is a crucial tool for supporting investors in making informed decisions, managing risks effectively, and strategically planning investments. Nevertheless, the challenges posed by the non-linearity and non-stationarity of time series data make it difficult to achieve precise predictions. This research introduces a novel hybrid deep learning prediction model for financial time series forecasting, based on the encoder-decoder architecture. Initially, the model utilizes the bidirectional gated recurrent unit (BiGRU) as the core unit for both the encoding and decoding processes. This allows the model to capture both short-range and long-range dependencies in financial data while maintaining a simple structure, reducing the number of parameters, facilitating fast training, and utilizing both forward and backward temporal information. Subsequently, the model incorporates an attention mechanism (AM) to emphasize relevant features and minimize the effect of irrelevant data during the decoding phase. To further enhance the model's performance, a skip connection (SC) is added to directly link the encoder and decoder, ensuring better utilization of hierarchical features during decoding. Finally, a three-layer fully connected neural network (FCNN) is integrated to extract key features and generate forecasting results. Experimental findings reveal that this proposed hybrid model surpasses other benchmark models in predicting three distinct time series from three different financial markets: stocks, cryptocurrencies, and commodities. Specifically, it achieves a substantial reduction in forecast error compared to the best-performing existing benchmark model (BiGRU-AE-AM), with the mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) for stocks (Microsoft, Apple, Google) reduced by 57.44 %, 48.20 %, and 66.87 %, respectively; the MAPE for cryptocurrencies (Ethereum, Bitcoin, Cardano) reduced by 71.59 %, 53.42 %, and 64.76 %, respectively; and the MAPE for commodities (Natural Gas, Crude Oil, Gold) reduced by 66.64 %, 72.56 %, and 48.63 %, respectively. Similarly, the mean absolute error (MAE) and root mean squared error (RMSE) for the proposed model show substantial improvements, indicating superior forecasting accuracy. Moreover, the effectiveness of this model is statistically validated using the modified Diebold-Mariano (MDM) test. The contribution of each sub-module within the proposed framework is also verified through ablation experiments. Furthermore, the model demonstrates its robustness by successfully adapting to major market events, such as the economic stimulus measures introduced during the COVID-19 pandemic and the geopolitical instability arising from the Russia-Ukraine conflict. This innovative method holds significant promise for assisting investors in achieving substantial and profitable returns.
