KW  - Adaptive fine-grained feature enhancement network
KW  - Channel-attention and local-spatial Aggregation
KW  - Dual-branch attention fusion module
KW  - Non-contact health monitoring
AB  - Calf diarrhea is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in newborn calves in the dairy industry. Traditional contact-based diagnosis is cumbersome and may induce stress responses. To achieve efficient and non-contact early identification, a machine vision-based calf diarrhea detection method named Adaptive Fine-grained Feature Enhancement Network (AFFENet) was proposed in this study. Based on the You Only Look Once (YOLO) backbone, a small-object detection layer at the finest feature scale, was introduced to enhance the extraction of feces region features. A Channel-Attention and Local-Spatial Aggregation (CALSA) module and a Dual-Branch Attention Fusion Module (DBAFM) further strengthen representation of fine-grained targets such as feces and calf postures. A calf diarrhea image and video dataset covering various weather and background conditions has been independently constructed for practical farming environments. On the test set, AFFENet achieved Precision (P), Recall (R), and F1-score (F1) of 0.889, 0.836, and 0.861, with the F1-score 7.6 % higher than YOLOv12n. For the Diarrhea class, F1 increased by 18.9 %, and the mean Average Precision (mAP) at 0.5 and 0.95 intersection over union thresholds was enhanced by 12.8 % and 18.4 %, respectively. The inference speed was measured at 147 frames per second (FPS), satisfying real-time monitoring requirements. In video data validation, an Accuracy of Classifier (ACC) of 90.67 % under sunny conditions and 87.32 % under rainy conditions was achieved, demonstrating good real-time performance and environmental adaptability. This method is expected to provide an efficient and accurate solution for the intelligent monitoring and early intervention of calf diarrhea.
