AB  - Connectors are vital industrial components whose quality directly influences the reliability and stability of electronics manufacturing systems. Ensuring accurate anomaly detection is therefore critical for product performance and manufacturing efficiency. Traditional rule-based and experience-driven inspection methods, however, often fail due to the scarcity, diversity, and complexity of defective samples. To address these limitations, this work proposes a novel diffusion-model-based deep learning framework for anomaly detection in computer connectors. Unlike reconstruction-error paradigms, the method adopts predicted noise vectors as discriminative features, refined through dimensionality reduction and a soft-voting ensemble of classical classifiers. The framework further integrates convolutional downsampling at high resolution, enabling the preservation of fine structural details essential for identifying subtle defects. Extensive experiments on multiple connector defect datasets, including Pin Twist and Surface Damage, demonstrate excellent performance. The proposed framework achieves up to 0.9875 accuracy and 0.9884 macro-F1 on Surface Damage, and 0.9789 accuracy with 0.9808 macro-F1 on Pin Twist, consistently outperforming conventional machine learning, state-of-the-art deep learning, diffusion-based and recent industrial baselines. The framework also proves highly effective under small-sample conditions and robust across input resolutions and modalities. These results establish diffusion-based modeling as both an innovative and practical solution for industrial anomaly detection, with strong potential for deployment in real-world manufacturing environments.
