AB  - The quality of monitoring images in underground mines are severely degraded by insufficient lighting and dust conditions, posing a significant challenge to coal mine safety monitoring and the application of visual intelligence systems. This paper presents a multi-perspective perception decomposition and fusion (MPPDF) framework to address the aforementioned issues. Specifically, we first design an adaptive perception enhancement (APE) method based on the camera response model and Retinex model to improve light sensitivity intensity. Secondly, contrast enhancement and color adjustment are achieved through channel reshaping based on contrast limited adaptive histogram equalization (CLAHE). Finally, the edge-preserving multi-scale decomposition fusion (EMDF) strategy is designed to achieve high-quality fusion of different perspectives while preserving sufficient detail information. Both qualitative and quantitative experimental results demonstrate that MPPDF significantly enhances the visual quality of low-light and dust-affected images in underground mines. and also demonstrates its effectiveness in two related tasks, i.e., low-light image enhancement and single image dehazing. In addition, object detection experiments have demonstrated that MPPDF is more helpful for monitoring the safety production of coal mine personnel and has great potential in advanced computer vision tasks.
