AB  - Understanding the gene expression profile of cancer from histopathological images has been a key challenge in cancer research because it is inherently hampered by limitations in the ability to understand the tumor microenvironment and to guide treatment decisions. The promise of spatial transcriptomics, the generation of non-local measurements of in situ transcriptomics at cellular resolution, allows us to bridge information on tissue morphology with this molecular information. However, current approaches often struggle to jointly interpret heterogeneous imaging modalities. To bridge this gap, we propose a deep learning-based cross-modal learning method, CrossToGene. Its main characteristics lie in the following three major innovations: a) a novel spatial encoding method that learns to capture both the macroscopic structure and microscopic information of tissue in an integrated manner; b) a bidirectional cross-modal interaction module for strong feature fusion from image feature and gene expression pattern; c) contrastive learning that directly aligns visual space and molecular space. We also validate CrossToGene on three cancer public datasets and obtain Pearson coefficients of 0.4225, 0.4523, and 0.3814 for highly expressed genes, respectively, which are 5.6 %–9.4 % higher than the second-best performance. Importantly, the model exhibited remarkable generalizability across independent cohorts of lung adenocarcinoma, colorectal cancer, and pancreatic adenocarcinoma, making it a potent novel candidate for elucidating tumor microenvironment heterogeneity and the development of precision cancer therapy.
