AB  - Event cameras asynchronously output time pulses by detecting changes in light intensity, providing valuable complementary cues for RGB (red, green, blue) cameras in extreme environments. However, existing methods commonly adopt a serial structure for feature extraction and interaction, where complementary features undergo shallow information stacking rather than deep semantic fusion, severely weakening the potential of inter-modal information complementarity. Additionally, the global dense attention employed by conventional transformers is structurally incompatible with the inherent sparsity of event modality, which can lead to noise interactions and compromise feature discriminability. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel dual-branch transformer tracking framework with complementary fusion attention (CFA) and sparse spatial channel attention (SSCA). Specifically, CFA is proposed to perform intra-modal self-correlation and cross-modal feature interaction within a unified attention model. By leveraging the complementary characteristics of RGB and event modalities, CFA directionally reinforces effective features and suppresses noise interference. For the inherent sparsity of event modality, a two-stage SSCA is proposed. Sparse spatial attention in the first stage employs a dual-stream parallel structure, embedding a sparse attention stream into the original dense attention stream to suppress invalid visual information in noisy regions. Channel modulation attention in the second stage captures inter-channel dependencies to suppress redundant feature channels and further enhance feature discriminability adaptively. Extensive experiments on three public RGB-Event tracking datasets demonstrate that our method outperforms state-of-the-art trackers and achieves superior performance.
