AB  - Reinforcement learning (RL) has been increasingly applied to local path planning for mobile robots. However, in locally observable dynamic environments, RL algorithms often face low sample efficiency and unstable convergence due to limited information and high uncertainty. To address this issue, we propose a novel RL method that requires neither expert knowledge in reward design nor manual intervention during training. Our method introduces an Advantage-Shaped Reward (ASR) function incorporating the advantage term A(s,a) as a penalty within a potential-based reward-shaping framework. We prove that ASR preserves the optimal policy while enhancing sampling efficiency as validated in both discrete and continuous environments. In the discrete environment, we propose a novel model-based asynchronous RL method that divides multiple asynchronous agents into explorers and a learner, effectively separating environment exploration from policy learning to improve sample efficiency. In the continuous environment, we integrate the ASR function into the TD3 algorithm and evaluate the method in the Gazebo. Our method significantly outperforms traditional reinforcement learning approaches, as evidenced by improved Success weighted by Path Length (SPL) metrics. In static environments, SPL rises from 0.78 to 0.88, and in dynamic environments, it increases from 0.62 to 0.73, reflecting better path quality and exploration efficiency.
