KW  - Early classification
KW  - Industrial alarm floods
KW  - Transformers
KW  - Temporal feature encoding
KW  - Hybrid networks
AB  - Alarm floods in industrial plants, triggered when process variables deviate from safe thresholds, can overwhelm operators and hinder timely responses, leading likely to catastrophic consequences. Existing alarm flood management approaches, including conventional sequence classification and alignment methods, offer limited early warning capability, do not fully exploit alarm timing and frequency, and rely heavily on preprocessing steps such as delay timers for chattering alarm removal, which constrains their effectiveness. To address these limitations, this paper presents a neural network framework for early classification of industrial alarm floods, explicitly designed as an engineering solution deployable in plant alarm management systems. The framework combines (i) an autoencoder that extracts low-dimensional latent representations and reduces correlations among process variables; (ii) a modified Transformer classifier operating in the latent space of the autoencoder, enabling efficient modeling of alarm patterns with reduced redundancy; (iii) an optimal time-encoded histogram that converts variable-length alarm sequences into fixed-length features capturing the timing and frequency of alarm activations. Learned exponential decay multipliers emphasize the temporal order of alarms; the fixed-length nature of this representation ensures compatibility with neural networks and robust learning across variable-length sequences. The encoder and Transformer components are jointly optimized in an end-to-end manner, ensuring full adaptation of the hybrid architecture to the early classification task. The proposed methodology is evaluated on the Tennessee Eastman Process dataset and compared against the state-of-the-art methods, demonstrating higher accuracy from the very first minutes of flood identification in the imbalanced classification setting.
