AB  - Accurately identifying high-quality online reviews is crucial for both consumers and e-commerce platforms. Consumers often exhibit confirmation bias when evaluating the helpfulness of product reviews. However, few research explores this psychological phenomenon when predicting multimodal review helpfulness. To address this gap, we propose a hierarchically trusted multi-view deep learning approach based on both the elaboration likelihood model and confirmation bias theory. Specifically,we extracts an initial belief view, central route view and peripheral route view from review text, images and metadata, respectively. A trusted multi-view fusion strategy is proposed to aggregate these cues considering confirmation bias. We evaluated the proposed method on datasets collected from the Amazon and Yelp platforms. Experimental results demonstrate that the approach achieves a mean average precision (MAP) value of 85.48 %, outperforming the best-performing baseline by 1.01 %, validating its generalization ability across platforms and product categories. These findings provide guidelines for e-commerce platforms to dynamically evaluate review helpfulness from multiple perspectives.
