KW  - Semantic segmentation
KW  - Atrous spatial pyramid pooling
KW  - Effective receptive field
KW  - Medical imaging
KW  - Urban scene understanding
KW  - Aerial image segmentation
AB  - DeepLab is a widely used neural network for semantic segmentation. Its success largely comes from atrous spatial pyramid pooling (ASPP), which aggregates multi-scale context through parallel atrous convolutions. However, the common practice of using fixed atrous rates limits the field of view (FOV) to a single size. We propose practical guidelines for choosing atrous rates. First, we observed that the use of the ASPP module yielded a specific pattern in the effective receptive field. We analyze this pattern to explain the module’s underlying mechanism. From this analysis, we derive a simple rule that sets atrous rates as a function of input resolution. Applying this matched rate improves performance across datasets for vessel segmentation, urban scenes, and aerial images, as well as across convolutional neural networks and transformer backbones.
